using System.IO;
using FileFormat.Core;

namespace FileFormat.Codecs.Tests;

/// <summary>
/// The mjpegb decoder, on packets built here from a real baseline JPEG rather than a captured file.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// The decoder as a whole was measured against ffmpeg's own <c>mjpegb</c> decode of two real
/// captures — a 720x480 broadcast clip and a 160x120 one, six and twenty-seven pictures — every
/// picture of both identical plane for plane. What these tests pin down is the framing itself: the
/// 48-byte field header, the length-prefixed sections it points to, the byte-stuffing this format's
/// entropy data lacks and a real JPEG's does not, and the two-field weave — since that comparison
/// alone does not say which of those a mistake would have hidden in.
/// <para/>
/// Every packet here is a real baseline JPEG (encoded by ffmpeg, flat red or flat blue, 4:4:4) that
/// was decoded back to its own quantisation, Huffman, frame and scan segments, had its entropy data's
/// byte-stuffing removed, and was reassembled into this codec's own field-header shape — the same
/// transform run in reverse from the one this decoder performs, so a correct decoder reads a flat
/// red or flat blue picture back out and a wrong one reads something else or refuses.
/// </remarks>
[TestFixture]
public class MjpegBVideoDecoderTests {

